Morning,

I need help with something that sounds simple but probably is not.

The resilience plan lists systems, but customers experience end-to-end servic=
es that cross ATM, card, transaction and communication processes. Failure ane=
cdotes are beginning to drive decisions, although retries and late posting ma=
y be making the problem look larger than the affected-customer population. Th=
ere are enough data exceptions to make a polished average dangerous; preserve=
 the unresolved cases.

The questions I would ask in the room are:
1. What would we expect to see if manual workarounds create later reconciliat=
ion breaks?
2. What evidence would instead support that recovery restores authorisation b=
efore posting catches up?
3. How do we rule out that cash access degrades before account servicing?

This has returned because the previous answer was useful but not strong enoug=
h to become a standing rule. Use October 2021 as the new evidence point rathe=
r than recycling the earlier conclusion. Please work towards the order in whi=
ch critical services should be recovered and tested. A useful output would be=
 a Power BI page with drill-through to a reproducible case list.

Use transaction-level timestamps, channels, amounts, statuses and error conte=
xt; ATM attempts, host responses, PIN attempts, blocked-card state and latenc=
y; customer contacts, complaints and suggestions; debit-order mandates, colle=
ction dates, suspensions and cancellations. One caution: Treat this as a hist=
orical service-impact rehearsal, not proof of infrastructure capacity.

